Parotitis
Opthalmology & ENT
Tender parotid swelling, Purulent Stensen's duct discharge, Trismus/fever

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: OET-032
Reason for Visit: Rapid, painful swelling of the face accompanied by high fever and severe inability to open the mouth or swallow fluids.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient only
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage reported an acute, severe, tender unilateral facial swelling with systemic fever and significant trismus. Urgent assessment was required to rule out deep neck space infection, Ludwig's angina, or impending airway compromise, and to initiate immediate systemic antibiotics to treat presumed acute suppurative parotitis prior to clinical deterioration during the after-hours period.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: "The side of my face blew up yesterday, it is burning hot, and I cannot open my mouth enough to drink water without excruciating pain."
History of Present Illness: Symptoms began insidiously 48 hours prior with generalized malaise and poor oral intake, rapidly escalating over the last 12 hours into an acute, tender, and distinctly visible swelling located anterior and inferior to the right ear. Patient reports high-grade fever, systemic chills, and generalized marked malaise. Endorses severe exacerbation of localized pain explicitly during chewing or swallowing. Trismus is profound, physically limiting voluntary mouth opening. Notes markedly diminished oral intake over the last 24 hours leading to dark urine and systemic dehydration.
Functional Status: Weakened, bedbound due to fever, highly distressed by the trismus.
Social/Safety Concerns: Living alone; high risk for worsening dehydration and localized abscess formation.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 110/65 | HR: 115 | Temp: 38.9 | RR: 18 | SpO2: 98% | Wt: 70 Kg
General: Acutely unwell, flushed, visibly diaphoretic, holding the right side of the face.
Physical Exam: Pronounced, exquisitely tender, and firm induration of the right parotid gland, with surrounding localized skin erythema and warmth. Swelling obscures the angle of the right mandible. Significant trismus limits mouth opening to 15mm. Intraoral examination reveals an erythematous right Stensen's duct orifice; gentle external glandular massage explicitly yields a drop of thick, purulent exudate. Pronounced preauricular and cervical reactive lymphadenopathy is present on the right. Airway is patent, uvula midline, no submandibular bogginess.
Environmental Observations: Empty water glasses on the bedside table; patient attempting to drink but failing due to pain.
Medication Reconciliation: Checked against EHR. Patient recently completed a course of anticholinergics for overactive bladder, contributing to profound xerostomia.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Acute Bacterial Suppurative Parotitis - Status: Acute, Severe
Differentials:

Ludwig's Angina - Ruled out/unlikely due to: The swelling being localized strictly to the parotid space (anterior/inferior to the ear) rather than bilateral submandibular and sublingual spaces, and the tongue is not elevated or displaced posteriorly.

Mumps (Viral Parotitis) - Ruled out/unlikely due to: The strictly unilateral presentation, the presence of expressible purulent exudate from Stensen's duct, and severe localized erythema (viral is typically bilateral, non-suppurative, and less erythematous).

Dental Abscess - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Lack of focal fluctuant gingival swelling, normal dentition on brief inspection, and the primary swelling clearly involving the anatomical boundaries of the parotid gland.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: Moderate (Living alone with severe dehydration risk)

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The patient presented with a rapid-onset, febrile facial swelling with trismus, immediately raising concerns for a dangerous deep neck space infection. Direct clinical assessment definitively localized the infection to the parotid gland and confirmed a bacterial etiology via the expression of purulent exudate from Stensen's duct. The combination of high fever, systemic tachycardia, and severe trismus-induced dehydration required immediate, aggressive intervention. By directly ruling out airway compromise, the visit prevented an emergency intubation scenario and allowed for the immediate commencement of high-dose empirical antibiotics and targeted hydration strategies in the home environment.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Prescribed oral Amoxicillin/Clavulanate 875/125mg BD for 10 days. Administered oral Paracetamol 1g and Ibuprofen 400mg STAT via a syringe to bypass trismus. Advised immediate cessation of all anticholinergic medications.
Positive Behavioral Change: To mechanically flush the infected gland and resolve the stasis, the patient must place a sour lemon drop or sip of pure lemon juice in their mouth every 2 hours while awake. The immediate, involuntary surge of saliva mechanically dilates the duct and flushes out the purulent debris (positive reinforcement via pressure relief), actively reversing the glandular stasis that caused the infection.
Home-Based Services (Optional): None.
DME/Safety (Optional): None.
Follow-up: GP review in 24-48 hours. If trismus worsens or fever persists >48 hours, requires immediate ED transfer for IV antibiotics and CT neck.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

Immediate ED presentation if the patient develops difficulty breathing, the swelling spreads under the chin to the opposite side, they cannot swallow their own saliva, or if they stop passing urine entirely for 12 hours.

PATIENT INFORMATION

Disclaimer: Diagnosis is clinical; severe cases may require intravenous antibiotics or surgical drainage if unresponsive to oral therapy.
Clinical Course: The extreme pain and fever should begin to respond to antibiotics within 48 hours. The swelling will take up to a week to fully resolve. Hydration is the most critical factor for recovery.
SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE

If your breathing becomes noisy, or if the swelling moves under your chin and pushes your tongue up, you must call an ambulance immediately as your airway is threatened.
PATIENT EDUCATION

Treatment Explanation: A lack of saliva caused bacteria to travel backwards up the saliva tube into your cheek gland, creating a severe infection. The antibiotics will kill the bacteria, but you must constantly stimulate saliva flow to wash the dead bacteria out.
Actionable Steps: Use sour hard candies or lemon juice frequently to force saliva production. Drink small sips of water continuously. Massage the swollen cheek gently from the ear toward the mouth while applying a warm compress.

